<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Wari Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 1000 CE (approx.) · capital Wari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first expansionist state of the Andean highlands, which built a network of administrative centres and roads across Peru centuries before the Incas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wari arose around 600 CE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> basin of the central Peruvian highlands, drawing on the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huarpa-culture">Huarpa culture</a> and religious imagery shared with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tiwanaku">Tiwanaku</a>. Its capital, Wari, grew into a city of perhaps 40,000 people covering several square kilometres.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From its capital Wari established planned provincial centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pikillacta">Pikillacta</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracochapampa">Viracochapampa</a> in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul">Cerro Baúl</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquegua">Moquegua</a>, facing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tiwanaku">Tiwanaku</a>. These rectangular compounds suggest a centralised administration that controlled labour and goods. Wari built terraces and roads that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> later reused, and may have used early khipus for records. Its tapestry tunics and colourful pottery spread a common style. Tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-castillo-de-huarmey">El Castillo de Huarmey</a> show the wealth of Wari noblewomen.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Wari state broke up around 1000, perhaps owing to drought and internal conflict, and its centres were abandoned. Its administrative model and roads influenced later states, notably the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, while regional powers such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chanka-confederation">Chanka Confederation</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor</a> rose in its wake.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wari-empire.jpg" alt="Wari Empire" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: QQuantum · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Wari_and_Tiawaku.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 1000 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Staff God cult</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Possibly Quechua or Aymara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Huari, Wari</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=800&amp;focus=wari-empire" aria-label="Wari Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2548 2527 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wari Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2868 2783L2951 2985M2868 2783L2881 2884"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2951 2985h0M2881 2884h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2868 2783h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2868 2783h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=800&amp;focus=wari-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

